WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.580
From their breakout in the 1970s, the Muppets
have been a cultural touchstone for so many.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.960
There's never a bad time to appreciate the
ways in which they have continued to impress

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.940
and engage an adult audience, despite their
appearance as children's fare.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.260
Here are some things you might have missed
as a kid.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:21.800
The original The Muppet Movie is a brilliant
and fun origin story that, for the most part,

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:22.600
is timeless.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.960
However, there are some ways in which it is
very much a product of its time, such as the

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.380
running Hare Krishna joke.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:33.800
Hare Krishna is a religious movement founded
in 1966 and a natural fit for those within

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:34.780
the hippie movement.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.780
In other words, it was the perfect punchline
for a 1970s comedy.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:43.149
The Muppet Movie opens with Kermit the Frog
sitting on a log in a swamp, happily playing

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:43.760
his banjo.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:49.060
He's interrupted by a man, played by Dom DeLuise,
frantically paddling a canoe and exclaiming

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.700
that he's lost his sense of direction.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.420
Kermit immediately suggests…

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:53.880
“Have you tried Hare Krishna?"

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.940
And, thus, the gag begins.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.440
Not long after, Kermit is observing Fozzie
Bear's stand-up routine and mutters to himself,

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.620
"This guy is lost."

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.370
The barkeep behind him says,

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.160
"Maybe he should try Hare Krishna."

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.620
At this point, Kermit breaks the fourth wall
and verbalizes what everyone is thinking.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.540
"Good grief, it's a running gag."

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:16.120
Finally, after driving for hours and hours
on their way to make it big in Los Angeles,

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:19.890
Kermit and Fozzie pull over at a run-down
church for a well-deserved rest.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.210
The sign outside the church says, "Lost?

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.940
Have you tried Reverend Harry Krishna?"

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.940
completing the gag and observing the comedic
rule of three.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.759
While the adults in the audience would be
aware of this movement, it's unlikely that

00:01:30.759 --> 00:01:33.310
kids would catch any of the Muppets' religious
jokes.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.880
"They don't look like Presbyterians to me."

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:40.300
Ever since they first arrived in the '70s,
the Muppets have asked their audience to be

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:43.450
somewhat conversant with the tropes of TV
and movies.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:47.280
And while kids might totally understand that
the sketch "Pigs in Space" is a reference

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.780
to Star Trek, they're less likely to see the
similarities to Lost in Space, especially

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.860
if we're talking about modern-day audiences.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:59.060
However, even kids back in the '70s probably
wouldn't get that the character of Dr. Strangepork

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.880
is named for the Stanley Kubrick film Dr.
Strangelove.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.320
These homages to classic films and TV shows
might be lost on younger viewers but they've

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:07.620
continued for decades.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.640
Most recently, the Alien franchise was referenced
during a "Pigs in Space" sketch, where Pepe

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.100
the King Prawn surprises the crew and pops
in for a guest appearance.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:19.500
"I don't know who you are, buddy, but your
stomach is a dump.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.640
You eat like a pig ---.”

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.940
Seeing as how the Muppets became popular in
the 1970s, it makes sense that references

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.390
to rock and hippie culture are baked into
many of the characters, most notably the members

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:33.320
of Dr. Teeth and the Electric Mayhem.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:38.840
This ragtag group of musicians includes bassist
and singer Floyd Pepper, whose name is a tribute

00:02:38.849 --> 00:02:42.910
to the British rock band Pink Floyd, as well
as the Beatles' album Sgt.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:44.460
Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:49.319
On top of all that, guitarist and singer Janice,
with her lethargic, spaced-out voice, drooping

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.340
eyelids, and bell-bottom jeans, is a stereotypical
stoned flower child.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.780
“Oh wow, wait until I tell the guys in the
band."

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.630
And during their performance of the song "Can
You Picture That?" in The Muppet Movie, the

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:04.890
band helps disguise Kermit and Fozzie's car
by painting it in psychedelic rainbow colors

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.910
— very typical of them but more perplexing
to Kermit.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:12.810
With their eccentric personalities, the Electric
Mayhem surely appeal to kids, but their specific

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.950
callback to free love and rock music might
have been more obvious to children of the

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:17.540
'70s.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.720
The Muppets have never shied away from joking
about the birds and the bees.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.319
Of course, these references were never lewd
and always between fully mature frogs, pigs,

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.020
chickens and whatever Gonzo is.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:32.280
One of the few instances of the Muppets overtly
spelling things out was during the opening

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.230
sequence of an early pilot show, which had
a very suggestive title.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:40.400
Then there's the entire episode of The Muppet
Show with Raquel Welch which ramped the sauciness

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.780
factor up to 11.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:45.680
There was a dance number with an eight-legged
freak, an intimate serenade with a smitten

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:50.040
Fozzie Bear, and a duet between Welch and
Piggy about the power of womanhood.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.500
The episode even includes some implications
about Kermit and Piggy's private life.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:59.049
"Tonight we have an internationally-acclaimed
actress, singer, dancer, and one of the sexiest

00:03:59.049 --> 00:04:00.420
ladies alive!”

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.580
“You get that every night, frog."

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:08.140
Also, one of the Muppets most popular characters,
Animal, is a walking, grunting, drumming embodiment

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:12.670
of the id, acting on all his most base needs
without consideration or remorse.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:14.959
He has a penchant for exclaiming "woman!"

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.280
any time he sees a member of the opposite
sex he finds appealing, and he wastes no time

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:20.460
before chasing them around.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.380
And who can forget Gonzo and his never-ending
attraction to chickens?

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:28.590
After many years, it seems that Gonzo has
finally settled down and is currently cooped

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.820
up in a long-term relationship with Camilla.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.860
The beaky romantics are clearly in love with
each other...even though some of their peers

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:36.620
seem to think of them as an odd couple.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.320
"A beautiful thing between a man and woman,
but really weird between a blue roommate and

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:41.960
a chicken."

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:42.460
"Whew."

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:47.600
The Muppets have always loved their guest
stars, but while some cameos are aimed towards

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.500
children, others were purely for grown-ups.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:53.710
That's especially true when it comes to The
Muppet Show, the classic TV series that featured

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:55.940
a new celebrity guest every episode.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:59.940
While kids back in the day definitely would've
appreciated watching Sylvester Stallone or

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.420
Mark Hamill, chances are pretty good that
they would've been baffled to see Russian

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:08.060
ballet dancer Rudolf Nureyev performing Swine
Lake with a human-sized pig.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.400
Another unexpected guest was Vincent Price,
a classic horror fixture, who allowed his

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.460
silliness to mix with his sinister side in
one of the show's Halloween specials.

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:21.310
Along these same lines, the Muppets welcomed
shock-rocker Alice Cooper, who, oddly enough,

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:25.680
felt like a perfect fit for the show with
his larger-than-life personality and flair

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:26.680
for theatrics.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:32.210
Plus, Rita Moreno, one of the few people to
win an Oscar, Emmy, Tony, and Grammy Award,

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:36.370
showed off her voice and perfect comic timing
in a musical stand-off with Animal.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:41.650
However, if kids of the '70s had trouble identifying
these stars, you can bet that modern-day children

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:44.479
would be completely baffled when revisiting
the show.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:47.980
But you never know, every so often you meet
a really cool kid who would have the perfect

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.560
reaction to any of these classic Muppet moments…

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:55.600
"That was awesome!" (laughter)"

